-**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Cross-Triangle Updates:** Barycentric coordinates are only differentiable within a triangle. If an update moves the embedding across a boundary, the gradient flow breaks unless the embedding is re-expressed in the neighboring triangle [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Mesh Accuracy:** Errors in segmentation or remeshing can produce artifacts, as the method relies on accurate semantic labels or mesh proxies [S1].
-*   **Degenerate Triangles:** Triangles with zero area can cause division by zero in scale computation.
+**Pitfalls:**
+*   **Cross-Triangle Updates:** Barycentric coordinates are only differentiable within a single triangle. If an optimization step moves the embedding point across a triangle boundary, the gradient flow is broken unless the embedding is re-expressed in the neighboring triangle. Failure to handle this causes Gaussians to stick to boundaries and pile up [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Mesh Quality:** The method relies on accurate semantic labels or mesh proxies. Errors in segmentation or remeshing can produce artifacts [S1].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
 ### GaussianMesh
-A method that defines Gaussians over an explicit mesh and ties the two via joint refinement: GS rendering guides adaptive face and Gaussian splitting, while mesh constraints regularize Gaussian shapes and support large, interactive deformations driven by mesh editing [S1]. It is chosen when interactive mesh editing is required.
+GaussianMesh is a variant that defines Gaussians over an explicit mesh and ties the two via joint refinement [S1]. Unlike SplattingAvatar which uses a predefined skeleton-based mesh (SMPL-X/FLAME), GaussianMesh uses GS rendering to guide adaptive face and Gaussian splitting, while mesh constraints regularize Gaussian shapes [S1]. It supports large, interactive deformations driven by mesh editing rather than just skeletal animation [S1].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Real-time rendering is achieved. Over 300 FPS on an NVIDIA RTX 3090 GPU and 30 FPS on an iPhone 13 [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Not given in the sources.
-*   **Strengths:** Explicit motion control by the mesh, compatibility with skeletal animation and blend shapes, high editability, real-time rendering capability [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Relies on accurate semantic labels or mesh proxies; errors can produce artifacts; editing attributes requires careful regularization [S1].
-*   **When to use:** When explicit mesh-based control of Gaussian deformation is needed, especially for human avatars.
-*   **When not to use:** When the scene does not have a clear mesh structure or when high-frequency details are not best captured by Gaussians.
+*   **Performance:** Achieves over 300 FPS on an NVIDIA RTX 3090 GPU and 30 FPS on an iPhone 13 [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Memory:** Memory usage is dominated by the Gaussian parameters and the mesh structure. The embedding parameters are lightweight per Gaussian.
+*   **Strengths:** Explicit motion control allows compatibility with various animation techniques (skeletal, blend shapes, mesh editing) [S2, unofficial]. Disentangles motion and appearance, allowing high-fidelity detail capture [S2, unofficial]. Real-time rendering capability in engines like Unity [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Relies on accurate mesh proxies or semantic labels; errors can cause artifacts [S1]. Requires careful regularization for editing attributes like texture and illumination [S1].
 
 ## Background
-SplattingAvatar, a prominent implementation of Mesh-Embedded Gaussian Splatting, was introduced by Zhijing Shao et al. in 2024 [S2, unofficial]. It builds on the 3D Gaussian Splatting technique [23] and Phong surface shading [38]. The method was developed to create photorealistic human avatars from monocular videos, disentangling motion and appearance. It has been ported to Unity for real-time rendering [S2, unofficial].
+SplattingAvatar was introduced by Zhijing Shao et al. in 2024 [S2, unofficial]. It builds on the 3D Gaussian Splatting technique [23] and Phong surface interpolation [38]. The method addresses the limitations of MLP-based deformation fields by providing explicit mesh-based control for human avatars [S2, unofficial]. It is designed to be trained from monocular videos and ported to real-time engines [S2, unofficial].
 
